Z||000017||Robert||10-27-2001||07:24 PM||||I just picked up a copy yesterday, but haven’t installed it yet.  I couldn’t pass up all the “freebies” that come with the professional version. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.officemax.com/max/solutions/microsite/windowsxp/windowsxpOffer.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=yes" TARGET=_blank>http://www.officemax.com/max/solutions/microsite/windowsxp/windowsxpOffer.jsp?BV_UseBVCookie=yes</A> <P><BR>Besides, the professional version handles new program installs/un-installs better.<BR>I’ll let you know how good the system is in a few days.<P>Robert<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000018||Cliff Pennock||10-27-2001||08:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B> How does that work?  Does that mean that I would get the better Linux OS but could still run all of my Windows programs?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, you'll get the stability of Linux and the programs of Windows. And if something <B>does</B> crash, it will not take everything else with it. Oh, and you'll suddenly have tons of free memory left.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||FieroMaster88||10-27-2001||08:26 PM||||Well, so far I have been pretty happy with XP.  It has been really stable so far and works really good for gaming.  I am happy with it.  <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000020||Heretic||10-27-2001||11:01 PM||||I wouldnt mind having XP but i dont even think it is worth it to get for my computer. My computer sux the big one!I'm probably going to stick with 98 until i upgrade.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://shopping.yimg.com/images/muze/album/390/399801_th.jpg">    <BR>TOOL OWNS j00!||||reg||1
Z||000021||Standard||10-28-2001||02:02 AM||||Buy XP?  hehehehe.  I've been running XP Pro for the last week or so...  I wanted to wipe and reinstall windows 98 SE so I figured might as well set up a dual boot 98-xp system.<P>So far I'm <B>really</B> impressed with XP.  It was a little unstable at first, but that's because I had my FSB at 142 mhz... dropped it down to 136 and it's rock solid.  I was quite surprised when I installed new drivers for my Kyro II and it didn't reboot.  I like all the extra security options it has over win 98.  It dropped my 3dMark scores a few hundred, but runs better overall with the better memory management.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||10-28-2001||05:53 AM||||Ill stick to win98. I recently went ahead and got a new laptop with ME. After having it lock up over and over it finally locked up completely and it wouldnt even accept reloading the configuration disk. I took it back and got a refund, had more memory added to old one instead.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Monkeyman||10-28-2001||06:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B> Yes, you'll get the stability of Linux and the programs of Windows. And if something [b]does</B> crash, it will not take everything else with it. Oh, and you'll suddenly have tons of free memory left.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What will I lose?  And why will I have tons of free memory?  Can I assume that I'll have to delete my current OS?  Would I end up losing all of my current programs?  And will it work with IE6, Outlook Express and all forms of Internet access (dial up and cable specifically)?<P>Should I be addressing these questions directly to Lindows.com?||||reg||1
Z||000024||btoth||10-28-2001||10:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>I have Windows 2k, and I absolutely love it!  I don't have any USB stuff, so I don't have to worry about the lack of drivers.  I love it so much because it WILL NOT crash.  I've only ever had the computer freeze up once in over a year of abuse.  I also love the ability to monitor the system so easily by opening the task manager.<P>Oh, and why in the heck would you rush out and BUY XP?  You mean, people actually pay for windows products?  I can't believe it....no way....is that true?  I don't have any problem with windows, but I don't know anybody that's ever bought it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What problems with USB?  I have so many USB devices and haven't even needed the drivers that come with them because Windows already has them installed.  One of my only complaints is that Win2k doesn't come with ASPI drivers.  How dumb is that?  I had to download some 3rd party ASPI drivers to get some of my programs working.||||reg||1
Z||000025||kslish||10-28-2001||11:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>Apple should port OS X directly to the PC and compete with Windows just for the heck of it.  It'd be funny.  I'd buy it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, Darwin (the variant of BSD Unix that forms the core of OS X) will run on a PC and is freely downloadable software.  It's just Aqua, OS X's graphical interface that won't.  But even that could probably be ported over to the PC, you'd just lose the backwards compatability with non OS X Mac software and some drivers would have to be rewritten. <P>However, a full version of OS X for PC will probably never happen, since Micro$oft wouldn't like that.  Microsoft holds the Mac versions of Internet Explorer and M$ Office like proverbial guns to Apple's head, basically threatening to stop development of those pieces of software if Apple ever competes head to head with Microsoft.  However, Microsoft feels free to copy whatever they want from Apple and incorporate it into their own OS.  Monopoly?  Nahhhh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000026||ray b||10-28-2001||01:36 PM||||heard on sharky extreme THAT EVEN SOME P4 MOTHER BOARDS WILLNOT WORK with bill's new toy.<BR>check your's before spending to see if it works on your setup. or wait for updates and price drops.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||11
Z||000027||theogre||10-28-2001||02:29 PM||||WXP? I wouldn't touch that diseased pile of consumer fraud if it were the last OS on Earth.<P>Product activation... Lotus 123 had an embedded SuperLock copy protection years ago. It drove everyone nuts. XP’s Product Activation is even worse. Only Microsoft’s largest customers qualify for the version that doesn’t have activation.<P>The licensing is abysmal... Forced upgrades every 2 years. This is a nightmare for any business. It can take 2 years just to get all the bugs out of the custom applications most large companies use. At that rate they will NEVER have stable applications again.<P>PassPort... If you are stupid enough to believe Microsoft isn’t going to have major security problems with this, you deserve whatever you get. Microsoft and Security are mutually exclusive terms. If you even dumber and think Microsoft won’t eventually start mining that data then you are very, very, naïve.<P>How many people here have actually ever read a privacy policy anyway? They mean nothing. There is no way to enforce them. Even if they were enforceable, companies commonly change them at will to suite their needs, usually WITHOUT telling people who agreed to the old policy. Example, Many polices have recently been altered to allow the sale of the entire database to another company should the present owner fold or merge. At least Wrenchead had the decency to give customers a way to opt out of the sale of that database to NAPA. They are one of the very few who have done that.<P>Add all that to the Raw Sockets security issues on the home versions and you are headed for some of the worst Internet worms you’ve ever seen. (www.grc.com for details.)<P>Windows XP is one of the WORST things to happen to the computing industry in the last decade.<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
